WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.079
An inbound entry point decides what happens when someone calls or texts one of your numbers.

00:00:07.079 --> 00:00:11.422
This video shows why it exists, and how to create one for calls,

00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:15.950
set its hours and voicemail, answer texts, and copy an entry point.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.125
A phone number does nothing on its own.

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:27.418
When a customer dials it, someone has to decide who answers, and what

00:00:27.418 --> 00:00:29.650
happens at night or on a holiday.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.590
Without one place for those decisions, the rules end up scattered:

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.420
one setting on the number, another on the queue, another in the phone system.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.291
An inbound entry point is that one place.

00:00:43.291 --> 00:00:48.471
It is the front door of one or more numbers, and it answers one channel:

00:00:48.471 --> 00:00:51.260
either voice calls or text messages.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.687
Numbers come from the Omnichannel Addresses list.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:02.852
An entry point picks the numbers it answers, and a number has one

00:01:02.852 --> 00:01:06.020
enabled entry point for calls and one for texts.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.488
Routing says where the call goes:

00:01:09.488 --> 00:01:13.181
to a queue, to one specific agent, or to an AI.

00:01:13.181 --> 00:01:17.710
Priority lets this number's calls jump ahead of other work.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.077
Hours say when the entry point is open, and what happens to a

00:01:22.077 --> 00:01:24.290
call that rings while it is closed.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.850
Voicemail says what a caller hears before leaving a message, and whose inbox receives it.

00:01:35.031 --> 00:01:36.653
Numbers come first.

00:01:36.653 --> 00:01:40.871
Open Interaction Center, Management, Omnichannel Addresses.

00:01:40.871 --> 00:01:45.609
Each address is one phone number your business owns, such as a main

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:47.491
line or a support line.

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:50.580
Click Add to see how a number is made.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.060
Give it a Name and the Phone number.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.429
Under Used for, tick Voice calls for a number that takes calls, Text

00:01:57.429 --> 00:02:00.322
messages for one that takes texts, or both.

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:08.762
Open Inbound entry points and click Add inbound entry point.

00:02:08.762 --> 00:02:11.904
A dialog asks what the entry point answers.

00:02:11.904 --> 00:02:14.469
Click Add on the Voice calls card.

00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:18.509
On the General card, type a Name, such as Support line.

00:02:18.509 --> 00:02:21.359
Under Numbers, pick the numbers it answers.

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:24.151
Only numbers ticked for calls are offered.

00:02:24.673 --> 00:02:28.181
On the Routing card, Route to is Queue by default.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:31.751
Pick the Target queue, Support, and set a Priority.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:36.573
Calls to this number then go ahead of lower priority work in the queue.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:37.980
Click Save.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.164
Calls to the number follow these rules straight away, and the entry point

00:02:42.164 --> 00:02:43.540
appears in the list.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.081
Edit the entry point, and open the Hours card.

00:02:51.081 --> 00:02:54.508
The Business hours calendar says when it is open.

00:02:54.508 --> 00:02:56.751
Left empty, it is always open.

00:02:56.751 --> 00:02:59.430
Here it follows the weekday calendar.

00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:04.608
Closed action says what happens to a call that arrives while closed.

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:07.328
Hold in queue keeps the call waiting.

00:03:07.328 --> 00:03:09.353
Voicemail takes a message.

00:03:09.353 --> 00:03:11.947
Overflow sends it to another queue.

00:03:11.947 --> 00:03:13.655
Reject ends the call.

00:03:14.109 --> 00:03:17.057
Pick Voicemail, and write a Closed message.

00:03:17.057 --> 00:03:21.569
It is spoken to the caller first, and then the closed action applies.

00:03:25.869 --> 00:03:30.318
On the Voicemail card, the Default voicemail greeting is spoken when the person

00:03:30.318 --> 00:03:33.929
receiving the message has not recorded a greeting of their own.

00:03:34.406 --> 00:03:39.956
Deliver voicemail to chooses An agent's inbox, or The queue's shared voicemail box,

00:03:39.956 --> 00:03:43.510
which any entitled agent or supervisor can pick up.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.126
Pick an agent under Voicemail inbox, and click Save.

00:03:51.337 --> 00:03:54.663
A number can ring one person instead of a queue.

00:03:54.663 --> 00:03:58.727
Add another call entry point, and name it Sales direct line.

00:04:01.085 --> 00:04:02.408
Pick its number.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:06.558
Then set Route to to Specific agent, and pick the Target agent.

00:04:06.558 --> 00:04:09.145
The call never falls back to a queue.

00:04:13.698 --> 00:04:17.856
Ring window sets how many seconds the agent's phone rings.

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:23.443
Send unanswered calls to voicemail, which is ticked, takes a message afterwards.

00:04:23.443 --> 00:04:24.548
Click Save.

00:04:28.748 --> 00:04:34.008
Add an entry point again, and this time click Add on the Text messages card.

00:04:35.082 --> 00:04:37.595
The General card works the same way:

00:04:37.595 --> 00:04:39.390
a Name, and the numbers.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.262
Only numbers ticked for texts are offered.

00:04:43.276 --> 00:04:45.835
On Routing, pick the Target queue.

00:04:45.835 --> 00:04:51.785
Queue distribution is Shared pool, where one agent claims each conversation, or Routed,

00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:54.536
which gives it to an available agent.

00:04:55.101 --> 00:04:59.704
Auto-reply is sent back to a contact who texts, at most once a day.

00:04:59.704 --> 00:05:04.812
On the Hours card, Closed auto-reply is sent instead, while you are closed.

00:05:04.812 --> 00:05:07.271
The text still reaches the queue.

00:05:09.528 --> 00:05:14.086
A number with no enabled text entry point still receives texts.

00:05:14.086 --> 00:05:16.728
They land in the unassigned inbox.

00:05:21.028 --> 00:05:26.580
The list shows every entry point with the channel it answers and its numbers.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.657
Edit changes one, and Delete removes it.

00:05:29.657 --> 00:05:34.808
A disabled entry point answers nothing, so untick Enabled to pause one.

00:05:35.313 --> 00:05:41.059
To start from an existing entry point, open its Actions menu and choose Clone.

00:05:41.059 --> 00:05:45.780
The form opens with every setting copied, and the name Copy of.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:51.253
Numbers starts empty, because each number has one entry point per channel.

00:05:55.453 --> 00:06:00.543
Add the number under Omnichannel Addresses, and create the queue it should reach.

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:04.723
Create the entry point for the number, with its hours.

00:06:05.473 --> 00:06:08.852
Have an agent sign in to the queue, and call the number.

00:06:08.852 --> 00:06:11.523
The call should be offered in My workspace.

00:06:12.273 --> 00:06:15.295
Keep the Live dashboard open while you test.

00:06:15.295 --> 00:06:20.313
The waiting count goes up first, then the agent moves from available to busy.

00:06:24.513 --> 00:06:28.368
A main line that rings the support queue in office hours, and takes

00:06:28.368 --> 00:06:30.323
voicemail the rest of the time.

00:06:31.073 --> 00:06:36.883
A personal line for an account manager, ringing one person and then taking a message.

00:06:37.633 --> 00:06:42.693
Separate numbers for sales and support, with sales at a higher priority.

00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:48.223
A text line that answers at once, and tells customers when you will reply.

00:06:50.223 --> 00:06:54.839
Add your numbers, create an entry point for each one, and choose its

00:06:54.839 --> 00:06:56.898
queue, hours and voicemail.

00:06:56.898 --> 00:06:59.831
Callers and texts then follow your rules.

00:06:59.831 --> 00:07:01.453
Thanks for watching.

